A||||5||Richard Parnell||V8 cam affecting brake booster||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||08-17-2000||02:05 PM||||I've been advised that I shouldn't keep the cam I have in my V8 because since it's not stock it will affect the vacuum boost for the brakes.  Anyone know anything about this?||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||08-17-2000||03:09 PM||||does the car stop now? if not than go to <A HREF="http://www.jegs.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jegs.com</A>  and get their free catalog.. and order a vacuum canister.. POOF problem fixed.. lots easier then a cam swap.. you need a pretty lofty cam to effect the braking system... do you have vacuum or mechanical secondaries on the carb? if your vacuum secondaries work you don't need more vacuum if they don't just ad a touch of nitrous.. that will make vacuum under the carrb and force the secondaries open.. just add a parachute to the car to help you stop  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> who needs brakes!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||08-17-2000||04:15 PM||||Thanks- one of the guys put a vacuum canister in to solve that problem.  He also has installe a larger truck master cyl for the brakes.  Another guy with a V8 with a lopping cam also has the larger master cylinder but no vacuum canister.  He has to stand on his brakes to stop.  I'll probably be installing the canister.  I really don't want to go to the expense of taking the engine apart.  It's a good running engine and don't want to mess it up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||08-17-2000||08:09 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.summitracing.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.summitracing.com/</A>  <A HREF="http://www.pawinc.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pawinc.com/</A>  <A HREF="http://www.wrenchhead.com" TARGET=_blank>www.wrenchhead.com</A> <P>just a few places to help save you a FORTUNE! and a bit of agrivation... <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||08-17-2000||08:32 PM||||I have the 345hp crate engine in my GT.When I first run the engine my brakes are a little weak.As soon as I get on the road and take my foot off the gas I have all the vacuum I need.It sounds as if you don't have power brakes,because if you do you already have a vacuum cannister with your master cylinder.Cams with a lot of overlap have less intake manifold vacuum,but as soon as you close the throttle at speed you have plenty of vacuum.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||08-17-2000||11:24 PM||||A cam with a lot of duration can effect vacuum accessories. What are the specs on your cam? If you have a very noticeable rumpity-rump lope at idle, you might have some trouble. If your duration is over 220 degrees at 0.050", then a reserve tank is probably a good idea. Over 225 @ 0.050" might not run a power brake booster at all.||||reg||1
